# KORVANE COLD CHAIN Sp. z o.o.

# EMPLOYEE HANDBOOK — VERSION 4

HR-HB-04 · Effective 1 February 2024 · Supersedes Version 3 (1 September 2021) in its entirety · Document owner: People & Culture · Classification: INTERNAL — may be shown to prospective employees on request

*Controlled document. If you are reading a printed copy, assume that it is out of date. The live version is at Intranet > People Desk > Policies > Handbook. Track-change residue from the review rounds has been removed as far as we could find it, apologies for any that remains.*

\pagebreak

## Foreword (from the Head of People & Culture)

Colleagues,

A handbook is not a thing anybody reads for pleasure, and I have no illusions that this one will be the exception. It exists because a company with five sites, three languages on the shop floor and roughly a dozen different kinds of contract needs one place where the rules are written down, so that two managers in two depots do not give two different answers to the same question at two in the morning. If the document does that job, it has done well; if it also occasionally saves somebody a phone call to People & Culture, so much the better.

Version 4 is the fourth edition. The first appeared in 2012, when there were fewer than thirty of us and the whole thing fitted on four pages; a second edition followed in 2016, shortly after the first pharmaceutical contract made us grow up a little; the third was issued on 1 September 2021 and was written, honestly, in a hurry. We have since opened Rotterdam, taken over the Kłaipėda yard, retired the KC-Tag 3 and moved the dispatchers into Tidewalk, so a number of the old rules had stopped describing reality. Version 4 was approved by the Management Board on 11 January 2024 (resolution 2024/01/07), after the Employee Council had been consulted on 19 December 2023, and applies from 1 February 2024.

Where this Handbook is more generous than the law, please do not assume the generosity is permanent or that it will survive a change of circumstances; where it is less generous than the law, the law of course wins. I am grateful to Sofia Brandão in Legal, to the depot managers who read the drafts at times of year when they had no business reading anything, and to the night shift ("the owls"), who sent back the most useful comments, and the rudest.

Ines Baptista-Okafor
Head of People & Culture

## Section 1 — Introduction and Scope

1.1 **Who this Handbook applies to.** This Handbook applies to every person employed by Korvane Cold Chain Sp. z o.o. (the "Company") under a contract of employment governed by Polish law, whether at the Gdynia head office and depot (GDY-1, "the Mothership") or elsewhere in Poland. It applies, with the modifications set out in the local addenda in Annex C, to employees at the Hamburg-Waltershof depot (HAM-2, addendum A-DE), the Rotterdam-Maasvlakte depot (RTM-3, addendum A-NL), the Gothenburg-Arendal depot (GOT-4, addendum A-SE) and the Kłaipėda trans-shipment yard (KLJ-5, addendum A-LT). In case of conflict between this Handbook and the mandatory law of the country in which the employee works, the local law prevails, and the relevant addendum records the differences we know of.

1.2 **Who it does not apply to.** Self-employed contractors, consultants and the staff of agencies are not covered, with two exceptions: Section 7 (Conduct) and Section 13 (Health and Safety) apply to everybody who enters a Company site, whatever their status. Temporary agency workers placed through our two staffing partners are covered by the client rules of the relevant agency agreement, which in practice mean the same things in different words.

1.3 **Status of the Handbook.** This Handbook is a statement of Company policy. It is not itself a contract and the Company may amend it, after consulting the Employee Council where the law requires, by publishing a new version with at least 14 days' notice. Individual contracts of employment prevail over the Handbook to the extent they are more favourable to the employee. Where this Handbook is silent, People & Culture decides, applying the principle of equal treatment.

1.4 **Language.** The Handbook is maintained in Polish and English. The Polish text is the authentic one. The German, Dutch, Swedish and Lithuanian summaries held by the depots are for convenience only.

1.5 **Our values.** The Management Board has asked us to state the four values once, in this section, and then leave them alone: keep it cold; keep it honest; keep each other safe; leave the yard tidier than you found it. They were chosen by a vote of the depot managers in October 2023 after a long evening and a short list that included "No excursions, no excuses", which was rejected on the ground that it would look good on a mug but bad in a courtroom.

1.6 **Grades.** Operations employees are graded O1 to O6, office employees G1 to G8 and managers M1 to M3. The grading schedule and its pay bands form Annex A and are updated every March. Pay bands for the operations grades are also reproduced in the contract template ET-OPS-2024/2.

## Section 2 — Employment Basics

2.1 **Probation.** New employees are engaged on a probation contract of 3 months, which continues as an indefinite contract if neither party terminates it. During probation either party may give 5 working days' notice. The line manager holds a review meeting with the new employee in the sixth week and again in the eleventh week; the results are recorded on form PD-40 (yes, the same form as used for informal discipline, it is a long story involving a printing contractor and a misread requisition). A probationer who has not received a second review by the end of week twelve is, in the view of People & Culture, a probationer who has been badly managed, and the Company will not rely on the absence of a review to dispense with the contract for the "wrong" reasons.

2.2 **Working time — office staff.** Office employees work 40 hours a week, Monday to Friday, with eight hours a day as the standard. Start of work is flexible between 07:00 and 10:00; core hours, when everybody is expected to be reachable, are 10:00 to 15:00. A break of 15 minutes, counted as working time, is granted where daily work exceeds 6 hours, and a further unpaid lunch break of 30 minutes is customary but not required by law. Office staff record their time in the Tidewalk timesheet and submit it weekly by Friday 16:00.

2.3 **Working time — operations staff.** Operations staff work on a shift roster in three shifts: morning 06:00 to 14:00, afternoon 14:00 to 22:00, night 22:00 to 06:00. The roster is published at least 14 days in advance; changes after publication need the employee's consent, save in a declared contingency. The settlement period for working time is four months. A night shift is any shift of which at least three hours fall between 22:00 and 06:00, and no employee may work more than five consecutive night shifts without the consent of the Depot Manager (and then not more than seven). Time is recorded at the badge terminal at the gatehouse.

2.4 **Overtime.** Overtime requires prior instruction from the shift supervisor (operations) or the Head of Department (office). Operations overtime is paid at 150% of the hourly base rate for the first 2 hours in a working day and at 200% from the third hour, and at 200% for all hours worked on a Sunday or public holiday; overtime between 22:00 and 06:00 is paid at 175% in total, the night supplement not being paid on top. The night supplement for ordinary night hours is 22% of the hourly base rate. The employee may instead choose time off at 1.5 hours per overtime hour. The annual cap on overtime is 156 hours. Employees in management grades M1 to M3 receive no overtime pay; their salary is understood to include it.

2.5 **Pay.** Salary is paid on the 10th of the month following the month worked, by bank transfer; where the 10th falls on a weekend or public holiday, payment is made on the preceding working day. Payslips are sent by e-mail in encrypted form. Pay is reviewed once a year in March, with effect from 1 April. Employees with a bonus entitlement are told their target in writing; for operations grades the discretionary bonus target is 6% of annual base pay, paid with the April payroll.

2.6 **Changes of personal details.** Address, bank account, family status and emergency contact must be updated on the People Desk within 7 days of the change. The Company cannot be responsible for a payslip sent to an old address or a tax form filed under a name that is no longer the employee's.

2.7 **Employment of relatives.** Relatives (spouses, partners, parents, children, siblings) may work in the same depot but not in the same reporting line; the CEO's approval is required for any exception. The Company has several married couples on its books and has never had occasion to regret it.

## Section 3 — Annual Leave

3.1 **Entitlement.** Annual leave accrues by reference to completed years of service. For a full-time employee: 0 to 2 completed years, 21 working days; 3 to 5 years, 24 working days; 6 to 9 years, 27 working days; and 10 years or more, 30 working days per leave year. Service with an acquired entity counts. Unpaid leave of more than 30 consecutive days does not count towards the years of service. The figures in Version 3 of this Handbook (20, 23, 26 and 28 days) are obsolete; nobody's entitlement is reduced by the change, since every tier is higher.

3.2 **The leave year** is the calendar year. In the year of joining, one twelfth of the annual figure is credited for each full calendar month, rounded up to the half day. In the year of departure the same method applies in reverse. Part-time employees are credited pro rata. Leave may be taken in units of half a day.

3.3 **Requests.** Request through form PD-12 on the People Desk. Absences of more than 5 consecutive working days must be requested at least 10 working days in advance; others at least two working days in advance. The manager responds within 4 working days. If the manager has not responded, the request escalates to the Depot Manager or Head of Department automatically. An employee must not book non-refundable travel before approval; the Company does not compensate for it (but see 3.7).

3.4 **Peak-season restriction.** Between 10 November and 23 December no more than one in five members of an operational shift team at GDY-1 or RTM-3 may be on annual leave simultaneously, unless the Depot Manager gives written consent. The restriction exists because citrus and pre-Christmas volumes arrive together, and because in the winter of 2022 a Gdynia night shift once went down to four people, one of whom was on a first-week induction. It is applied informally at HAM-2.

3.5 **Carry-over.** Up to 6 days may be carried into the next leave year and must be taken by 31 May. Days above that limit are scheduled by the Company unilaterally, with notification of the dates no later than 30 November. Unused leave is not paid out during employment.

3.6 **Special paid leave.** Not deducted from annual leave: two days for the employee's own wedding; four days on the death of a spouse, child or parent; two days on the death of a sibling or grandparent; one day per calendar year for moving house (rental or purchase document required). Founders' Day, 15 May, is a half day: work ends at 12:00 for all employees not rostered on a live reefer-yard shift, who receive the half day as time in lieu. Care leave and other statutory days are granted as the law provides.

3.7 **Recall from leave.** An employee may be recalled from approved leave only in an emergency declared by the Head of Operations or a Depot Manager. The Company then reimburses documented non-refundable costs up to PLN 3,500 and the days not enjoyed are re-credited in full.

3.8 **Public holidays on Saturdays.** Where a public holiday falls on a Saturday, a day off in lieu is granted within the same settlement period. For operations staff the Depot Manager schedules it; for office staff the Company publishes a closure day in the holiday calendar (see Annex B for the yearly calendar).

3.9 **Unpaid leave and sabbatical.** Unpaid leave of up to 30 days per year may be granted by the Head of Department. After 7 completed years of service an employee may apply for a sabbatical of up to 12 weeks, unpaid, with a guaranteed return to an equivalent role; a sabbatical may be taken once in any period of 5 years.

## Section 4 — Sickness Absence and Sick Pay

4.1 **Notification.** An employee unable to attend must notify the shift lead (operations) or the line manager (office) by telephone not later than one hour after the scheduled start of the shift; office staff without a fixed shift must call by 09:30. A text message or a message through a colleague does not count, unless agreed beforehand with the shift lead. The employee is asked to give the expected length of absence, and to say whether any work in progress is at risk (open reefer alarms, a vessel call, a pharma pick-up).

4.2 **Certification.** An electronic certificate (e-ZLA) must reach the Company within 3 working days of the first day of absence. For absences of up to 2 consecutive working days the employee may self-certify, but not more than 4 days of self-certified absence are accepted in a calendar year; further self-certified days are treated as unauthorised absence.

4.3 **Sick pay.** The Company pays sick pay in steps counted cumulatively over each twelve-month period which begins on the first day of the first absence. Days 1 to 3: 100% of base pay. Days 4 to 33: 90% of base pay. Days 34 to 90: 88% of base pay for employees with at least 3 completed years of service, and statutory sickness benefit only for all others. From day 91 only statutory sickness benefit is paid. Version 3 paid 80% on days 1 to 3 and 85% on days 4 to 33, which is why a few older payroll tables still say 80.

4.4 **Welfare call.** On the fifth calendar day of any continuing absence the line manager shall make a welfare call, which is not a check on the employee but an opportunity to ask whether the employee needs anything. The call is recorded in one line on the employee's absence record.

4.5 **Return to work.** A return-to-work conversation takes place after any single absence of 10 or more consecutive working days and after the third separate absence in any rolling six months. It is conducted by the line manager on form PD-31 and is not a disciplinary meeting.

4.6 **Occupational health.** After 30 days of absence in any twelve months the Company may refer the employee to its occupational-health provider (Medis Pracy Sp. z o.o.) at the Company's cost. The doctor reports on fitness and adjustments only and does not disclose diagnoses to the manager.

4.7 **Sickness during leave.** Where an employee falls sick during approved annual leave and provides an e-ZLA, the leave days covered by the certificate are credited back, save for the first day.

4.8 **No sick pay.** Sick pay is not payable where the incapacity results from the employee's own intoxication or from a deliberate act, and the Company then applies only the statutory rule.

## Section 5 — Remote and Hybrid Working

5.1 **Eligibility.** Remote working is available for roles that do not require physical presence at a depot, to employees who have completed the 3-month probation period. Operations staff on a shift roster are not eligible. Dispatchers may work from home only in a contingency declared by the Head of Operations (for instance an official closure of a depot, or a flood warning for the Gdynia yard, as in January 2023, when four dispatchers worked from their kitchens for two days and one of them, we are told, dispatched a vessel call from a dentist's waiting room).

5.2 **Frequency.** Up to 2 days per week and not more than 8 days in any calendar month. Days of leave and sickness do not count as remote days.

5.3 **Office days and core hours.** Tuesday and Wednesday are core office days on which all office-based employees are expected at ul. Łużycka 14, unless on leave or travelling. Core hours remain 10:00 to 15:00 on every working day.

5.4 **Home-office allowance and equipment.** Employees who work remotely on at least 4 days a month on average receive a flat allowance of PLN 60 gross per month, which covers electricity, heating and internet and for which no receipts are required. A one-off equipment stipend of PLN 450 (for a monitor or a chair; receipt required) may be claimed once in 36 months. People & Culture also arranges an ergonomic assessment of a home workstation once, free of charge, on request.

5.5 **Work from abroad.** Permitted only in EU or EEA countries, for not more than 20 working days per calendar year, and only with written approval of the line manager and of People & Culture obtained not later than 15 working days before the first day abroad. The Company must assess tax, social-security and data-protection consequences before approving; employees who book flights first do so at their own risk.

5.6 **Security.** Remote work is performed on a Company laptop connected through the Company VPN. Public Wi-Fi must not be used without the VPN. No customer temperature data (KC-Tag exports) may be printed at home. Lost or stolen equipment must be reported to the IT helpdesk within 2 hours of discovery.

5.7 **Right to disconnect.** Outside the employee's working hours, and during leave, the employee is not expected to answer e-mail or chat messages, except where the employee is on standby under the contract. Managers who send messages at 23:00 are asked to schedule them for the morning, and to be ashamed.

## Section 6 — Business Travel and Expenses

6.1 **Claims.** Expenses are claimed on form EX-7 in the ColdLedger expense module (or, until the module is fixed, on paper) within 30 calendar days of the end of the trip. Later claims require the approval of the Finance Controller. Approved claims are paid with the payroll on the 10th of the month following the month of approval.

6.2 **Ceilings.** The following ceilings apply per person to receipted costs. They are ceilings, not allowances.

| Country | Meals, per day | Hotel, per night, breakfast incl. | Private car, per km | Parking, per day |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Poland | PLN 118 | PLN 430 | PLN 1.27 | PLN 65 |
| Germany | EUR 36 | EUR 128 | EUR 0.34 | EUR 18 |
| Netherlands | EUR 39 | EUR 146 | EUR 0.36 | EUR 22 |
| Sweden | EUR 41 | EUR 152 | EUR 0.33 | EUR 20 |
| Lithuania | EUR 29 | EUR 97 | EUR 0.27 | EUR 9 |

Swedish costs are paid in SEK at the ECB rate on the day of travel. For hotel rates in Hamburg, Rotterdam and Gothenburg during large trade fairs the Head of Department may approve an excess of up to 25% in writing before booking. The Version 3 ceilings (for example EUR 105 for a Hamburg hotel and EUR 0.29 per km in Germany) are obsolete.

6.3 **Receipts.** A receipt is required for every single item above PLN 65 (EUR 15). Alcohol is not reimbursed under any circumstances. Tips are reimbursed up to 10% of the bill where customary and shown on the receipt.

6.4 **Mode of travel.** Economy class for flights below 6 hours; second class for rail; taxis only between 22:00 and 05:00, or if the traveller is carrying luggage over 20 kg, or if there is no public transport to a depot (Kłaipėda yard at night, for instance). Company pool cars may be booked through Tidewalk from the Fleet team (Gunnar Halvorsrud's people), who will also ask you to fill the tank.

6.5 **Approvals.** A trip with an estimated cost above EUR 500 needs the Head of Department's prior approval; above EUR 2,500 the CFO also signs. Client entertainment is limited to EUR 70 per head and requires advance approval from the CFO's office.

6.6 **Advances.** On request an advance of up to 80% of the estimated cost of a trip can be paid, on form EX-9, and any excess over the actual costs must be repaid within 10 days of the claim. An employee with an outstanding advance older than 60 days has no further advance until the old one is cleared.

6.7 **Mileage records.** Private-car mileage is reimbursed against a log kept in Tidewalk showing date, route, odometer reading and purpose. Mileage to and from the regular place of work is never reimbursed.

6.8 **Company credit cards.** Corporate cards are issued only to grades O5, G5, M1 and above, with a monthly limit of EUR 3,000, and statements must be reconciled by the 5th of the following month.

## Section 7 — Conduct

7.1 **General standard.** Every employee is expected to behave with courtesy, honesty and respect towards colleagues, customers, port staff, drivers and visitors. The Company does not tolerate bullying, harassment, discrimination or victimisation, whether on the ground of sex, age, disability, religion, ethnic origin, sexual orientation or any other ground, in person, in writing or in group chats. Jokes that begin with "no offence, but" may be offensive.

7.2 **Complaints of harassment.** A complaint may be made to a line manager, to People & Culture, or through the Speak-Up channel (7.11). The Company acknowledges the complaint within 5 working days and appoints an investigator who is not in the complainant's reporting line.

7.3 **Alcohol and drugs.** No employee may report for work, or remain at work, under the influence of alcohol or drugs. A shift supervisor with reasonable suspicion may order a breath test, carried out by two persons trained for the purpose, and a re-test after 15 minutes. A reading above 0.10 per mille is gross misconduct (code GM-2). A reading between 0.01 and 0.10 per mille leads to removal from the shift without pay for the hours not worked and is treated as a Stage 1 conversation under Section 8. Prescribed medication that may affect safe working must be notified to the shift supervisor on a confidential basis.

7.4 **Gifts and hospitality.** Gifts from customers, suppliers or port-agents with a value above EUR 60 must be entered in the gift register kept by the Legal department. Gifts above EUR 150 must be declined or surrendered, and cash or cash equivalents are never accepted. Modest hospitality in the course of business is acceptable; a week in a ski chalet is not.

7.5 **Conflicts of interest.** An employee with a financial interest in a customer, supplier or competitor must declare it in writing to Legal within 10 working days of becoming aware of it. Secondary employment requires the written consent of the Head of Department and is refused where it overlaps with the employee's rostered hours or competes with the Company.

7.6 **Social media and press.** Employees must not post photographs of customers' cargo, of container seals, of the security arrangements at any depot, or of the screens of Tidewalk. Press and media enquiries are referred to Lotte Sandberg in Marketing. Employees who speak about the Company in a private capacity are asked to say so.

7.7 **Customer data.** Temperature, humidity, door-open and location data recorded by KC-Tag devices and transmitted through KC-Hub 200 gateways belongs to the customer and is confidential. Unauthorised disclosure to a third party is gross misconduct (code GM-4).

7.8 **Smoking.** Smoking and vaping are prohibited on all Company premises except in the marked shelters, and in no event within 15 metres of a fuel point or a battery-charging bay.

7.9 **Dress and PPE.** High-visibility clothing of class 3 is mandatory in every yard. Employees working in cold stores are issued a thermal jacket rated to -30 °C and thermal gloves, which are replaced free of charge on wear. Safety boots are required in yard areas; the Company reimburses PLN 380 for one pair per year if the employee buys boots that meet the specification (S3 with steel toe).

7.10 **Use of Company property.** Company equipment may be used for brief personal purposes provided that this does not interfere with work, entail cost or breach Section 14.

7.11 **Speak-Up.** Any person may report a suspected breach of law, of this Handbook or of customer requirements, confidentially or anonymously, by e-mail to speakup@korvane.example or by telephone to +48 58 555 0142. The Company acknowledges every report within 5 working days and gives feedback on the outcome within 60 days. Retaliation against a person who has reported in good faith is itself a disciplinary offence.

## Section 8 — Disciplinary Procedure

8.1 **Principle.** Discipline is meant to correct conduct, not to punish people, and it must be proportionate, prompt and documented. The Company applies a ladder of four stages. A stage may be skipped only with the approval of People & Culture, which will be given where the conduct complained of is serious enough that starting at the bottom would be absurd.

| Stage | Sanction | Stays on the personnel file |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Documented informal conversation (form PD-40) | 6 months |
| 2 | Written warning | 12 months |
| 3 | Final written warning | 18 months |
| 4 | Termination, with notice, or without notice for gross misconduct | not applicable |

8.2 **Timings.** A sanction may be imposed only within 10 working days after the line manager became aware of the conduct, and in no case later than 3 months after the conduct occurred. The employee is invited to a disciplinary meeting in writing at least 3 working days in advance, with a statement of the complaint, and may be accompanied by a colleague or a trade-union representative. If the employee does not speak Polish well, the Company provides an interpreter at its own cost. The decision is communicated in writing within 5 working days after the meeting. A written warning is signed by the Head of Department concerned; a final written warning and a termination are signed by the Head of People & Culture.

8.3 **Appeal.** An appeal is lodged with the Head of People & Culture within 7 calendar days of receipt of the decision. It is heard by a panel of two, one of whom is a member of the Legal department, and decided within 10 working days. The sanction takes effect in the meantime. The decision on appeal is final within the Company; the employee's rights before the labour court are not affected.

8.4 **Gross misconduct.** The following may lead to summary dismissal after a hearing: falsification of a temperature or door-open record (GM-1); reporting for a shift with a breath-alcohol reading above 0.10 per mille (GM-2); theft (GM-3); unauthorised disclosure of customer data to a third party (GM-4); deliberate disabling of a KC-Tag 4 or KC-Hub 200 alarm (GM-5); violence or threats of violence (GM-6). The list is illustrative of the standard and not exhaustive; other conduct that destroys the trust the employment requires may also be treated as gross misconduct.

8.5 **Investigation and suspension.** An investigation is to be completed within 15 working days of the line manager becoming aware; the Head of People & Culture may extend it once by 10 working days. Pending the investigation the employee may be suspended on full pay for up to 15 working days. Suspension is not a sanction.

8.6 **Records.** Records of sanctions are kept in the personnel file for the periods in the table. After the period the entry is closed, and may not be relied upon in later proceedings except to show a pattern of conduct, if it was a final written warning.

## Section 9 — Grievances

9.1 **Informal.** An employee with a grievance should raise it first with the line manager, who will try to resolve it within 5 working days.

9.2 **Formal.** If that does not resolve it, the employee submits a written grievance to the Head of People & Culture within 30 days of the matter arising (or of its last occurrence). Receipt is acknowledged within 3 working days. A meeting is held within 10 working days, and the written outcome follows within 5 working days after the meeting.

9.3 **Appeal.** The employee may appeal to the CEO, Marta Kowarzik, within 7 days of the outcome. The CEO's decision, normally given within 15 working days, is final within the Company.

9.4 **Confidentiality.** All persons involved are asked to keep the matter confidential. Nobody may be disadvantaged for raising a grievance in good faith.

## Section 10 — Family Leave

10.1 **Statutory leave.** Maternity, paternity, parental and childcare leave are granted as provided by the Labour Code. This Section describes only the Company's additional provisions.

10.2 **Welcome-baby payment.** An employee with at least 12 months of service receives a one-off payment of PLN 1,350 gross for each child born or adopted into the family.

10.3 **Second-parent leave.** An employee with at least 12 months of service who is the second parent of a newborn or newly adopted child receives 10 additional working days of paid leave, to be taken within the first six months after the birth or adoption, in addition to any statutory leave.

10.4 **Antenatal appointments.** Time off, with pay, for antenatal appointments and examinations, on presentation of an appointment card, for the pregnant employee and the second parent for one appointment per pregnancy.

10.5 **Phased return.** On returning from maternity or parental leave of more than 12 weeks an employee may work 80% of contractual hours for up to 6 months, paid at 90% of full-time base pay.

## Section 11 — Training and Development

11.1 **Personal training budget.** Each employee has a training budget of PLN 2,600 per calendar year, which does not carry over, and 3 paid working days of training leave per year. Requests are made on the People Desk and are approved by the line manager if the training relates to the current role or to a role the employee is credibly preparing for.

11.2 **Compulsory certification.** Courses and exams required for the role (for example the F-gas certificate for reefer technicians, ADR for drivers, and the Tidewalk certification for dispatchers) are paid by the Company in full, outside the personal budget, and taken in working time. A first re-sit is paid by the Company; a second re-sit is paid by the employee.

11.3 **Repayment.** If the Company pays for a course or certification costing more than PLN 4,200 and the employment ends within 18 months of the course's completion, other than by reason of the Company's fault, the employee repays a proportion of the cost which falls by one eighteenth for each full month elapsed since completion. The details are fixed in the employment contract and in a separate training agreement; the Handbook merely reminds.

11.4 **Language courses.** The Company subsidises language courses (English, German, Dutch, Swedish, Polish for foreign colleagues) at 70% of the fee, up to PLN 1,600 per calendar year.

11.5 **Mentoring.** Each October People & Culture pairs new joiners of the preceding twelve months with a volunteer mentor from another department in the "Cold Mentors" scheme. Mentors are given 2 hours a month in working time for the purpose.

11.6 **Performance conversations.** A structured performance conversation is held twice a year, in April and in October, and the notes are signed by both sides. Failure to hold a conversation is a failure of the manager, not of the employee.

## Section 12 — Termination and Notice

12.1 **Probation.** During the probation period of 3 months either party may terminate with 5 working days' notice.

12.2 **Notice by the Company.** After probation: 2 weeks if the employee has been employed for less than 6 months in total; 1 month if employed for 6 months up to 3 years; and 3 months if employed for more than 3 years. Under Version 3 the longest tier was 2 months.

12.3 **Notice by the employee.** One month in grades O1 to O4 and in the office grades G1 to G6; two months in grades O5 and above, in the office grades G7 and G8, and in all managerial grades M1 to M3.

12.4 **Garden leave.** The Company may release the employee from the duty to work during the notice period, with base pay continuing in full. Garden leave may be combined with the use of any remaining annual leave, in which case the employee is credited the days as taken.

12.5 **Return of property.** Badge, PPE, laptop, handheld tag reader and any other Company property must be returned within 3 working days after the last day of employment. The cost of unreturned items is deducted from final pay only within the limits of the Labour Code.

12.6 **Final settlement.** Final pay is settled by the 10th of the following month. Accrued unused leave is paid out at 1/21.5 of monthly base pay per day.

12.7 **Certificate and exit interview.** A certificate of employment is handed over on the last working day. People & Culture offers an exit interview in the last 5 working days. Notes of exit interviews are anonymised and reported to the Management Board twice a year.

12.8 **Non-competition.** Grades O4 to O6, and equivalent office and management grades named in the contract, may be bound by a post-termination non-compete of 9 months with compensation of 40% of the average monthly pay of the preceding 6 months. The Company may waive it within 14 days after notice is given.

12.9 **References.** References are given by People & Culture only, and state dates, position and, if the employee requests it, a short assessment signed by the line manager. Managers who give private references are asked to make clear that they speak for themselves.

## Section 13 — Health and Safety (summary)

13.1 **Where the rules are.** The detailed rules are in the Health and Safety Manual HSE-01, kept by Quality & Compliance (Benedikta Aaltonen). This Section is a summary for the benefit of the Handbook reader and does not replace the Manual.

13.2 **Cold exposure.** After 90 minutes of continuous work at -18 °C or lower, a warm break of 20 minutes must be taken. Nobody works alone in a freezer store; the buddy rule requires a second person within sight or radio call. The thermal allowance of PLN 4.35 gross per full hour at -18 °C or lower is paid under the employment contract.

13.3 **Night work.** No more than five consecutive night shifts without the Depot Manager's consent. Employees on night shifts are entitled to a hot meal voucher of PLN 22 per night shift.

13.4 **Medical examinations.** Occupational-medicine examinations are performed before the start of employment and then every 24 months, or every 12 months for employees who work regularly in cold stores. They are paid by the Company and conducted in working time.

13.5 **Accidents and near misses.** Every accident is reported to the shift supervisor immediately and recorded on form HSE-4 within 24 hours. Near misses are also reported, and nobody is disciplined for reporting one.

13.6 **First aiders.** There is to be one trained first aider for every 25 employees on each shift at each depot.

## Section 14 — IT and Data Use

14.1 **Support.** The IT Support Lead is Kamil Zdunek. The helpdesk can be reached at helpdesk@korvane.example or on extension 4400, and is staffed from 06:00 to 22:00 on working days; outside these hours an incident is raised in Tidewalk and picked up in the morning, unless it is marked "customer-impacting".

14.2 **Passwords and access.** Passwords have at least 14 characters and are never shared. Multi-factor authentication is mandatory for e-mail, Tidewalk and ColdLedger. Accounts are disabled on the last day of employment at 18:00, unless the employee is on garden leave, in which case they are disabled on the first day of garden leave.

14.3 **Incidents.** A suspected security incident (phishing click, lost device, wrongly addressed e-mail containing customer data) must be reported to the helpdesk within 2 hours of discovery.

14.4 **Devices.** Company laptops are replaced after 4 years and mobile phones after 3 years. Personal devices may be used for e-mail only through the managed mail application. Company phones are issued to grades O4, G4, M1 and above.

14.5 **Personnel records.** The Company keeps personnel files for 10 years after the end of employment. Employees have the right to inspect their file; requests go to People & Culture and are answered within 14 days. The Data Protection Coordinator is the Legal Counsel, Sofia Brandão.

## Section 15 — Benefits

15.1 The Company offers a health plan, a pension (employee capital plan) with a Company contribution that rises with tenure, and a number of perks (sports card, transport reimbursement, lunch and thermal-clothing support, tenure awards). Details, tiers and eligibility are set out in the Benefits Overview 2024 and are not repeated here.

15.2 For the avoidance of doubt, the Company contributes PLN 96 per employee per month towards the basic health plan regardless of the plan the employee chooses; the employee pays any difference.

15.3 Benefits are not part of the contract of employment unless stated in it. The Company may change the providers or the details with 30 days' notice.

## Section 16 — Document Control

16.1 **Version history.** Version 1: 2012. Version 2: 2016. Version 3: 1 September 2021. Version 4: 1 February 2024. The next scheduled review is on 1 February 2026; interim amendments are published as dated notices which form part of this Handbook until the next version.

16.2 **Ownership.** The document owner is People & Culture. Suggestions are sent to hr@korvane.example, telephone +48 58 555 0100, or entered on the People Desk under "Handbook feedback". Anybody who finds a typo is asked to report it, as the last reviewer did not.

